-- Milestone 1, step 1 (companion pet collection): pilot the Black Tabby Cat
-- through the new learn-once/summon-forever pattern. See /ARCHITECTURE.md
-- at the repo root for the full design.
--
-- Two new custom spells (reserved custom ID block 60000-60999, matching the
-- range the client-side "Pets" tab will later recognize - see
-- ARCHITECTURE.md):
--   60001 "Teach Companion: Black Tabby"  - SPELL_EFFECT_LEARN_SPELL (36),
--         triggers 60002. Cast directly by the item's on-use effect, never
--         itself learned by the player - same pattern as any stock recipe/
--         formula item (e.g. item 16247 "Formula: Enchant Weapon - Superior
--         Impact" -> spell 20082).
--   60002 "Summon Companion: Black Tabby" - SPELL_EFFECT_SUMMON_CRITTER (97)
--         pointing at creature_template 7383 ("Black Tabby"), identical
--         effect to the existing spell 10675 this companion already used.
--         This is the spell that gets permanently learned (character_spell)
--         and is what the player actually casts to summon/dismiss.
--
-- Item 8491 "Cat Carrier (Black Tabby)" (patch=4 row only - the patch=0
-- "Maine Coon" row is left untouched, out of scope for this pilot):
--   spellid_1: 10675 -> 60001 (now teaches instead of directly summoning)
--   spellcharges_1: 0 -> -1 (item is now consumed after exactly one use -
--     confirmed via src/game/Spells/Spell.cpp's TakeCastItem logic: negative
--     charges = "expendable", destroyed once the (single) charge is spent)
--
-- NOTE: 60001's spellIconId is left at 1 (the stock placeholder "?" icon,
-- SpellIcon.dbc) since it's never shown to the player (never learned, never
-- in character_spell - see above). 60002's spellVisual1/spellIconId (353/29)
-- are instead copied from the live DB's own spell 10675 "Summon Maine Coon" -
-- the stock summon spell this item used before milestone 1, still otherwise
-- untouched - rather than invented: confirmed via live testing that this is
-- also what gives the summon/dismiss cast its sound (the client plays the
-- cast's own visual/sound every time the spell is cast, on both branches of
-- the toggle, so one field covers both; a silent spellVisual1=0, this
-- migration's first version, was silent on both for the same reason).
--
-- `equippedItemClass` is -1 on both spells, not 0: confirmed from this
-- server's own src/game/Objects/Item.cpp (IsFitToSpellRequirements),
-- `EquippedItemClass != -1` is the "has a requirement" check, so 0 reads as
-- "must have a class-0 (Consumable) item equipped" - a requirement that can
-- never be satisfied, since consumables aren't equippable. An earlier
-- version of this migration left both at 0 (effectively copy-pasted as the
-- "empty" default) and both spells failed every cast with
-- SPELL_FAILED_EQUIPPED_ITEM_CLASS as a result - caught via live testing.

